-- lib/nbe.cub — typed codes Code(Γ, A) = Σ c. Der(Γ, c, A) over the one representation of terms
-- (lib/code.cub's Code), their evaluation, and normalization by evaluation with its soundness
-- equation (paper §6.1, (6.1) and (6.5)). Loaded after lib/std.cub.
--
-- Plan (2026-11-12). The whole construction, in the order it is built.
--
-- 0. Source of the method. Kripke normalization by evaluation for dependent types with a logical
--    relation (computability) over the category of contexts and order-preserving renamings: the
--    method of Altenkirch–Kaposi (LMCS 2017) and Coquand ("Canonicity and normalization for
--    dependent type theory", TCS 2019), and for the cubical part Sterling–Angiuli (LICS 2021).
--    The derivations are indexed by their meaning (McBride, "Outrageous but meaningful
--    coincidences", 2010): a derivation of c : A in Γ carries, as an index, the meaning of c as a
--    function of the environment. So eval is that index, the object theory's conversion is the
--    language's own judgmental equality on meanings (no conversion rule, nothing to make coherent),
--    and (6.5) holds by the indices.
--
-- 1. Objects, each defined once.
--    Raw     the existing Code (lib/code.cub); normal forms here use cvar, clam, capp, cpair, cfst,
--            csnd, ccase #0 (Bool), ccon #0 k, cplam, cpapp — the codes the language's own (code t)
--            writes for the same terms.
--    Types   (revised 2026-11-12, see the finding below) a type is a shape (TyShape, closed data)
--            with a closure: a base K, a map from the environment into K, and the shape's structure
--            over K, whose components are again closures (Clo, ElC). Its meaning ElC is computed by
--            recursion on the shape, so it is definitional at open environments; pulling a type
--            back along a map of environments (weakening, substitution) composes the map and is
--            definitional; no reindexing operation or law is needed.
--    Ctx     a context: a skeleton (list of shapes, closed data) with a closure per entry; its
--            environment type is computed by recursion on the skeleton.
--    Var, Der  variables and typing derivations, Der Γ D c v: c is a derivation-checked raw code
--            of type D in Γ with meaning v : (ρ : G) → T ρ.
--    Code Γ D = Σ c. Σ v. Der Γ D c v; eval = the meaning v (paper (6.1)).
--    Ne, Nf  neutral and normal derivations, same indices; embed : Nf → Der keeps c and v.
--    OPE     order-preserving embeddings Δ → Γ (the Kripke worlds), with actions on Var, Ne, Nf.
--    Comp D Δ δ x  the computability of x : (ρ : D) → T (δ ρ) at world Δ, by recursion on D:
--            Bool — a normal form with meaning x; Π — Kripke: at every later world, computable
--            arguments give computable results; Σ — componentwise.
--    reify/reflect  mutually by recursion on D: reify : Comp → Σ c. Nf with meaning exactly x;
--            reflect : Ne → Comp. The Π clauses are the paper's Kripke clauses (§6.1).
--    fund    the fundamental lemma, by recursion on Der: a derivation's meaning is computable at
--            every world under every computable environment.
--    normalize d = reify (fund d (reflected identity environment)); the result is a Code with the
--            same meaning index, so reifySound (6.5) is refl: eval (reify v) ρ ≡ denote v ρ.
--
-- 2. Phases. P1 the fragment Π, Σ, Bool (with dependent case): shapes and closures, Ctx, Var,
--    Der, Code, eval, Ne, Nf, embed, OPE and actions, Comp with its monotonicity (renaming)
--    law, reify/reflect, fund, normalize, reifySound. P2 PathP and the interval: worlds with
--    dimension variables and face restrictions (Comp at a path type is computability of every
--    dimension instance, with the boundary). P3 Kan operations, transp and hcomp, at Π, Σ, Bool,
--    PathP: Sterling–Angiuli's stabilization (neutrals carry the face on which they are unstable).
--    P4 Glue. P5 the specializer over Code (lib/specialize.cub's normalize, t11) — needs each
--    definition reflected into Der, which is the typed checker's output (goal typed-checker).
--    P6 universes: codes decoded by shape recursion at a fixed top level, smaller levels lifted
--    (see the correction below).
--
-- Correction (2026-11-12, replaces t45, closed): universes are not blocked and need nothing added to
-- the core. The object hierarchy U_0..U_k is finite, so it is interpreted at a fixed top meta level
-- (the Gödel stratification): one type-structure family at that level, smaller meanings lifted to
-- it. A lift is a Σ with a field at the target level filled by one fixed constant (Lift1 A = A ×
-- (Type → Type), checked); a `data ... : Type 1` annotation does not raise a level (a data type's
-- level is its fields' largest: `data Lift0 (A : Type) : Type 1 = lift0 (x : A)` is accepted but
-- `CtxL (Lift0 Unit)` at `CtxL : Type 1 → Type 2` is refused, "actual: Type (lmax lzero lzero)").
-- What lifting does not give is the round trip raise (lower x) ≡ x definitionally (the core has η
-- for Π and Σ, none for a unit type), so El (π a b) ≡ Π (El a) (El b) between a lifted code and the
-- top-level Π holds as an equivalence, not judgmentally. P6 therefore interprets each universe as
-- codes (shape and closure, as below) decoded by shape recursion, and states El's commutation with
-- the type formers as paths used by an explicit conversion rule at universe-decoded types.
--
-- Finding (2026-11-12) that fixes the design below: transport along a constant line of a variable
-- type does not reduce (CCHM has no regularity; `transport (λ i → A) a ≡ a` by refl is refused, while
-- at Bool it is accepted). The language's indexed families are forded (each index an equation field),
-- so a function defined by recursion over one computes its clauses only up to such transports when
-- the index involves variable types: `K (ty-bool G) D δ x` came out with `comp (λ i → D) [] ρ` in its
-- body. Everything whose clauses must compute inside types (the meaning of a type, computability,
-- environments of a context) is therefore defined by recursion on closed first-order skeletons
-- (TyShape, lists of shapes), where clauses compute definitionally; indexed families (derivations,
-- normal forms, renamings) are only ever the arguments of functions whose result types are fixed by
-- their signatures, so the transports inside their results are harmless (their indices, the
-- meanings, are fixed by the types).
--
-- Elaborator quirk found 2026-11-12: in a structural recursion, an implicit binder after the recursive
-- argument is not abstracted in the induction hypothesis (the inner call gets the outer value, and an
-- explicit {..} is ignored); such parameters are taken explicitly here.

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------- types: shapes and closures
data TyShape = s-bool | s-pi (a : TyShape) (b : TyShape) | s-sig (a : TyShape) (b : TyShape)
-- the structure of a Bool type: nothing; One1 is a type at level 1 with the one value `one`
data One1 = one1 (pad : Type → Type)
one : One1
one = one1 (λ X → X)
-- a shape's structures over a base K and their meanings
TyU : Type → Type 2
TyU K = (M : Type 1) × (M → K → Type)
-- a closure over environments K: a base K', the map into it, a structure over K'
CloU : (K : Type) → ((K' : Type) → TyU K') → Type 1
CloU K u = (K' : Type) × (K → K') × fst (u K')
ElCU : (K : Type) → (u : (K' : Type) → TyU K') → CloU K u → K → Type
ElCU K u A k = snd (u (fst A)) (snd (snd A)) (fst (snd A) k)
-- by recursion on the shape: Π and Σ have a domain closure and a codomain closure over the
-- environments extended by the domain
TyE : TyShape → (K : Type) → TyU K
TyE s-bool K = (One1 , λ D k → Bool)
TyE (s-pi a b) K =
  ((A : CloU K (TyE a)) × CloU ((k : K) × ElCU K (TyE a) A k) (TyE b) ,
   λ D k → (x : ElCU K (TyE a) (fst D) k) → ElCU ((k : K) × ElCU K (TyE a) (fst D) k) (TyE b) (snd D) (k , x))
TyE (s-sig a b) K =
  ((A : CloU K (TyE a)) × CloU ((k : K) × ElCU K (TyE a) A k) (TyE b) ,
   λ D k → (x : ElCU K (TyE a) (fst D) k) × ElCU ((k : K) × ElCU K (TyE a) (fst D) k) (TyE b) (snd D) (k , x))
-- a type of shape s over environments K, and its meaning
Clo : TyShape → Type → Type 1
Clo s K = CloU K (TyE s)
ElC : (s : TyShape) → (K : Type) → Clo s K → K → Type
ElC s K = ElCU K (TyE s)
